The {{Nihongo|'''Okami Times'''|大紙タイムズ|Okami Taimuzu|lit. "big paper"}}, stylized in Mercian as '''The Okami Times''', is a [[Sanese language|Sanese-language]] daily newspaper published in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. It is one of the oldest and most widely circulated newspapers in Okaiken, and is considered a {{w|Newspaper of record|newspaper of record}} for the nation.

The Okami Times is one of the five major national dailies of Okaiken, alongside the ''Okami Shimbun'', the ''Noto Keizai Shimbun'', the ''[[Toyozawa Mainichi]]'', and the ''Sanese Sankei''. Its morning edition circulation of 1.2 million makes it the second-largest newspaper in Okaiken behind the ''Okami Shimbun''. Since 2023, the newspaper has published a [[Mercian language|Mercian]] translation edition of its daily content, intended for the international diplomatic, business, and roleplay community.

Its publisher, the Okami Times Media Group, is a {{w|Privately held company|privately held company}} with its registered headquarters in [[Matsushika-ku, Okami|Matsushika-ku]], Okami.

==History==

===Early years (1947–1960)===
The Okami Times was founded on March 1, 1947, by Sanese journalist {{Nihongo|Hara Tetsuya|原哲也|}} as a small four-page broadsheet under the name ''The Okami Herald''. It was originally published in [[Mercian language|Mercian]] to serve the expatriate and diplomatic community in Okami during the final months of the Illypnian Theater and the early days of the Shendan conflict. The paper's first editorial, published on March 3, 1947, called for "a free press as the foundation of a free Okaiken."

In 1952, following the [[Armistice of Okami]] and the formalisation of the Etruscan security pact, Hara renamed the paper ''The Okami Times'' and transitioned to a bilingual Sanese-Mercian format, making it the first newspaper in Okaiken to serve both domestic and international readerships. The paper moved to an all-Sanese domestic edition in 1955, while maintaining a separate Mercian-language digest for the Etruscan military and foreign community.

By 1958, circulation had reached 180,000, driven by the paper's aggressive coverage of the emerging labor movement and the early LDP dominance.

===The Quiet Revolution and its aftermath (1960–1970)===
The Okami Times earned its reputation for fearless political reporting during the [[Quiet Revolution]] (1967–1968). The paper's investigative unit, the ''Tokubetsu Hōdōbu'' (Special Reports Section), was the first to report on the [[Genzui Kusaka|Kusaka]] government's secret legislation to abolish the Diet, publishing the leaked draft on November 2, 1967 — three weeks before it was formally introduced in the Diet. The scoop earned the paper international recognition and the {{w|Journalism award|Noto Prize for Journalism}}.

During the 1968 riots, the paper's Matsushika-ku offices were briefly occupied by OSP loyalists, and the printing presses were damaged. Hara defiantly published the next day's edition from a temporary facility in [[Aizawa Prefecture]], an event commemorated annually by the paper's staff as "Press Day."

Following the ban on the OSP, the Okami Times advocated for press freedom protections to be enshrined in the new Mitoyagi Republic constitution, a position that was ultimately adopted in the 1970 constitutional charter.

===Era of LDP dominance (1970–1990)===
Throughout the 1970s and 1980s, the Okami Times established itself as the leading centrist voice in Sanese media, often critical of LDP corruption but skeptical of leftist radicalism. Its editorial line under editor-in-chief {{Nihongo|Kaneko Satoshi|金子智|}} (1972–1988) was described as "pragmatic reformism" — supporting economic liberalization while demanding political accountability.

The paper broke the {{w|Political corruption|Miyazaki Hotel Scandal}} story in 1989, which led directly to the collapse of the LDP government and the brief PPO premiership of [[Shigehara Honda]]. The investigation, led by reporter {{Nihongo|Tsumura Keiko|津村圭子|}}, won the paper's second {{w|Journalism award|Noto Prize}}.

===Modern era (1990–present)===
The Okami Times adapted to the digital era more slowly than its rivals, but its reputation for in-depth reporting sustained its print circulation longer than most. The paper launched its digital edition in 2006 and its mobile app in 2012.

In 2023, the paper launched the Mercian Translation Edition — a full Mercian-language rendering of each day's Sanese-language content. This edition, published digitally and as a limited print run, was aimed at the growing international community, diplomatic corps, and overseas readers interested in Okaiken's political upheavals. The move was controversial internally, with some traditionalists arguing it diluted the paper's Sanese identity, but it proved commercially successful: digital readership quadrupled by 2026.

The paper's coverage of the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]], the [[Digital Sovereignty Act]], and the [[Kizan prosecution|Kizan Heavy Industries prosecution]] has been widely cited internationally, and the Mercian edition has become a primary source for non-Sanese-speaking observers of Okaiken politics.

==Political stance==
The Okami Times is considered centrist to centre-left, with a long tradition of investigative reporting on political scandals. It has been described as "the newspaper of the pragmatic center" — more willing to challenge the establishment than the conservative ''Sanese Sankei'', but more moderate than the left-leaning ''Toyozawa Mainichi''.

The paper supported the [[Honda Shigetsuma|Honda]] government's social programs, including the [[National Universal Health Act]], but was critical of its technology policy, particularly the Satori AI dependency that caused the Blackout. It endorsed the PPO in the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021]] and [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025]] general elections, but withdrew its endorsement for the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026]] election, citing "unresolved accountability questions" and calling for an independent inquiry into the Satori Blackout.

The Okami Times has historically advocated for {{w|Pacifism|pacifism}} in foreign policy and supported restrictions on autonomous weapons systems, positions that have drawn criticism from the conservative establishment.

==Sections==
The daily edition is organized into the following sections:
* '''Headlines''' (''Top News'') — Front-page political and national news
* '''Entertainment''' (''Culture & Arts'') — Culture, music, television, film, and literature
* '''International''' (''World'') — Foreign affairs and diplomatic coverage
* '''Sports''' (''Sports'') — O.League, OBSL, horse racing, combat sports, golf
* '''Editorial & Opinion''' (''Opinion'') — Editorials, columns, and left-leaning commentary
* '''Okami Region''' (''Metro'') — Okami Metropolis and surrounding Okami Region local news
* '''Regional''' (''Regional Briefs'') — Briefs from Okaiken's prefectures and municipalities

The paper's investigative unit, the ''Tokubetsu Hōdōbu'', publishes long-form investigative pieces in a dedicated weekend supplement.

==Mercian Translation Edition==
{{See also|The Okami Times/22 June 2026}}

Since 2023, the Okami Times has published a daily Mercian translation of its Sanese-language content. The Mercian edition is published digitally at en.okamitimes.co.ok and as a limited broadsheet print run of approximately 15,000 copies, distributed primarily to embassies, foreign businesses, and the Okami international district.

The Mercian edition uses the same section structure as the Sanese edition but with some localization: regional briefs include prefecture maps, and Sanese political terms are glossed with Mercian equivalents. The editor's note in each Mercian edition states: "This is a translation of the Sanese-language edition. Where ambiguity exists, the original Sanese text is authoritative."

Past editions of the Mercian edition are archived as wiki subpages at '''The Okami Times/{date}'''.

